Subject: [PATCH 1/6] moe gate: initialise the face lanes the kernel ranks but the gather never writes

`round_up_num_total_experts` did not exist; `glm_moe_router` passed
`num_total_experts=256` as a literal (`glm_moe_router/op.py:137` at HEAD) while
the gather filled only as many lanes as the gate MM produced. On a 64-expert
model the gate ranked 128 lanes over 64 written ones and the other 64 were
whatever the shared scratch arena last held.

WHY THE EXISTING -100 BIAS SENTINEL IS NOT ENOUGH

blaze's own reference tensor builder pads both halves —
`generic_moe_gate/op.py:92-93` fills the act face with `0.0` and the bias face
with `-100.0`. That is the contract, and it is a *pair*: the gate ranks
`act + bias` per lane, so bounding one side alone bounds nothing. The router
path had no act pad at all, and its logits are post-sigmoid, so real lanes sit
in (0, 1) and score at most ~1. Any stale bf16 word above ~100 in the tail
outranks every real expert even after the sentinel.

WHY IT PRODUCED GARBAGE RATHER THAN A FAULT

A winning padding lane yields an expert id in [num_experts, num_total_experts),
which has no weights behind it. `dram_streaming_matmul/kernels/op.hpp:182-191`
takes that id from the index CB and computes
`expert_offset_bytes = expert_idx * expert_size_bytes` with no bounds check. At
GLM-4.7-Flash's dims the per-bank expert stride happens to land the read inside
a *different real* weight tensor, so it is a legal DRAM read returning finite
numbers. MEASURED on silicon: `glm_moe_router` PCC 0.0235 — wrong, but neither
hanging nor faulting, which is what made it hard to attribute.

THE FIX

* `round_up_num_total_experts(num_experts)` — rounds to a kernel-supported
  block. The SFPU sorts whole 128-lane blocks and static_asserts
  `>= 128 && % 128 == 0` (`ckernel_sfpu_generic_moe_gate_topk.h:176-177`,
  `..._top8.h:178-179`), so an unrounded count is a *compile* error, not a slow
  path. Its docstring states the pad-both-sides contract.
* `gate_face_padding_plan(num_experts)` — pure-Python bookkeeping separating
  lanes *written* (`ceil(num_experts/32)*32`, a property of the gather: one
  32-wide page per gate-MM core) from lanes *ranked* (`num_total_experts`, a
  property of the kernel). Asserting on this needs no device.
* `make_gate_gather_dst(...)` — returns the existing `f.cb_scratch` CB unchanged
  when the two agree, and a zero-filled `f.named_tensor`-backed face when they
  do not. Three properties make the zeros persist where a scratch slot's would
  not: named tensors are pinned in the compiled program's `lifetime_tensors`
  (`blaze/compiler.py:317-329`) so the buffer is never re-let; the gather writes
  only `sender_idx * data_size_bytes` from the base
  (`gather/kernels/op.hpp:108`); and the one-page CB is popped every iteration
  (`generic_moe_gate/kernels/op.hpp:92`) so the write pointer returns to base.
* `GenericMoeGate.emit` now rejects an out-of-block `num_total_experts` in
  Python, naming the helper that fixes it, instead of letting it reach a
  static_assert inside a JIT build.
* Both GLM and DeepSeek-V4 routers derive `num_total_experts` from the plan.

NO-OP FOR EVERY SHIPPING CONFIG. At 128 and 256 experts the plan reports no
tail, `make_gate_gather_dst` returns the same `f.cb_scratch` with the same name,
page size, tile and core ranges, and `plan.num_total_experts == num_experts`.
GLM-5 and DeepSeek-V4 take a byte-identical path.

Also drops `pop_act=True` from both call sites. It is inert: the compute kernel
declares the CT arg at `generic_moe_gate/kernels/op.hpp:47` and that is its only
occurrence in the file — act and bias are popped unconditionally at lines 92-93.
The stale comment claiming it controlled popping the gathered CB is removed.
Retiring the parameter properly means deleting the kernel-side declaration too,
which this patch does not do.

STATUS
* Fix VERIFIED on silicon (Blackhole Galaxy, harvested 12x10 grid): with the
  zero-filled face the device selected expert 44, a real lane, where before it
  selected a padding lane.
* `tests/blaze/micro_ops/moe/test_gate_tail_padding_contract.py` is CPU-only
  (`pytest.mark.device("cpu")`) and NOT RUN ON HARDWARE — it needs no device. It
  reproduces the half-padded configuration through `GenericMoeGate.golden` and
  shows the sentinel failing, then passing once the act tail is zeroed,
  parametrised over values a live arena plausibly holds (1e4, bf16 max, NaN, and
  two values small enough that the sentinel alone would have coped).
* The DeepSeek-V4 router change is source-reviewed only; NOT RUN, we have no
  DeepSeek-V4 numerics gate here.

Apply before patch 2 of 6.
